You will be given a larger set of texts extracted from Twitter user activities
replies to a source tweet are not automatically about the same rumor just
because they're replying to a tweet that is. Treat every text you're given as
unscreened until you classify it below.
 
THE RUMOR: the false claim that an 8-year-old GIRL, a survivor of the
Sandy Hook Elementary School shooting, was killed in the Boston Marathon
bombing while running the marathon in tribute to the Sandy Hook victims.
The true fact is that the victim was an 8-year-old BOY, Martin Richard,
a spectator (not a runner) waiting for his father to finish the race.
 
TASK: Sort every text into exactly ONE of the following two lists:
 
1. DOUBTFUL (D)
   The text expresses uncertainty, hesitation, or a request for
   verification about the rumor -- it neither confirms nor denies it, but
   signals the author is unsure. Examples of the pattern (not exhaustive):
   "is this true?", "can someone confirm this?", "I hope this isn't real",
   "not sure if this is legit," hedged phrasing like "reportedly" or
   "apparently" used to flag uncertainty rather than to assert the claim.
 
2. IMMUNE (I)
   The text actively rejects, corrects, or debunks the rumor with
   confidence -- it asserts the rumor is false, not just questions it.
   Examples of the pattern: "this is fake," "it was a boy, not a girl,"
   "that's a sick and twisted rumor," "false, do not share this."
 
3. IRRELEVANT
   The text is about something completely unrelated to this rumor --
   general Boston bombing commentary, condolences, a different rumor
   about the same event, unrelated conversation, etc. IRRELEVANT means
   off-topic, nothing more.
 
CRITICAL EXCLUSION RULE:
   If a text simply REPEATS or SPREADS the rumor (states the false claim
   as fact, with no doubt and no correction), it is an INFECTED /
   rumor-spreading text -- this does NOT belong in DOUBTFUL, IMMUNE, or
   IRRELEVANT. Do not classify it at all. Leave it out of every list and
   do not mention it anywhere in your output. Only texts that either
   doubt the rumor, debunk the rumor, or are unrelated to the rumor
   should appear in your output.
 
RULES:
- Assign IMMUNE only when the text is confidently rejecting the rumor. If
  it merely questions or hedges without asserting the rumor is false, it
  is DOUBTFUL, not IMMUNE.
- Process every text given to you, but only output the ones that qualify
  for DOUBTFUL, IMMUNE, or IRRELEVANT per the definitions above. Infected/
  rumor-spreading texts are silently excluded per the rule above.
 
OUTPUT FORMAT: produce exactly three Python-style lists, using the full
text as each list element (not the tweet ID), like this:
 
doubtful = [
    "text 1",
    "text 2",
    ...
]
 
immune = [
    "text 1",
    "text 2",
    ...
]
 
irrelevant = [
    "text 1",
    "text 2",
    ...
]
 
Output only these three lists.
